    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d99">

                <pb facs="d98-1" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v07-09mSupp/d98-1"/>

                <head>99. Todis 35 to Geneva, March 12<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d99-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-head" calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1962-03-12T18:17:00-05:00">March 12, 1962, 6:17 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <pb facs="d99-1" n="1" type="facsimile" xml:id="d99-1"/>
                <p>UK idea of declaring that we will rely on
                    national detection systems when scientific developments permit would open door
                    to proposals for atmospheric test ban to be monitored by national systems. Since
                    our position is that Soviet rejection of treaty along lines of April 18 draft
                    constitutes rejection of President’s March 2 offer, we believe that <pb
                        facs="0436" n="285" type="typeset" xml:id="pg285"/> initial emphasis must be
                    placed on control system envisaged in that treaty. We would therefore not favor
                    adding proposed UK declaration.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed corresp="#p_BGW_1">Ball</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d99-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d99-f1-content">
                <p>Guidance on handling U.K. declaration on national detection systems.
                    Confidential. 1 p. Department of State, Central Files, 700.5611/3–1262.</p>
            </note>
